-- Moderation: a marketplace's listings — drafts discarded, sellers closing
-- their accounts, reports outliving what they report. The sixth Velle spec,
-- written to put OQ37's delete machinery on display in business terms:
-- `delete` as an ordinary rule effect, `undeletable` as the state-scoped
-- deletion gate, `? initially required` as the reference that absorbs its
-- target's deletion, and the per-field copy discipline for what must survive.
--
-- Comment convention (as in payments.velle): plain comments tell the business
-- story; `velle:` comments note the language machinery on show.

-- ── Sellers and listings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

expose shape Seller {
    name: text
}

-- A listing requires a seller at creation, but the reference is declared
-- absorbing: if the seller's account is closed — deleted — the listing
-- survives with `seller` reading as absent. Never-set is impossible (creation
-- required presence), so `seller is none` MEANS "the seller is gone."
-- velle: `? initially required` — read-side optionality and creation-side
-- requiredness as orthogonal declarations (OQ37-R10); reads ride the existing
-- narrowing machinery (`is none`, `is some`, `?.`)
expose shape Listing {
    title: text
    seller: one Seller? initially required
    isDraft: boolean initially true
}

never (Listing where title == "")

shape Draft = Listing where isDraft

-- Published listings cannot be deleted. The gate is negative and state-scoped
-- (deletion permission beside write permission), spelled on the complement
-- state exactly as a freeze would be — and with a boolean the complement is
-- exact, so the disjointness check covers the sentence fully.
-- velle: `undeletable`, the frozen-sibling deletion gate (OQ37-R1/R9); every
-- deleter below rides the `isDraft` complement, which the shared
-- refinement-overlap prover clears (checks.md V27)
shape PublishedListing = Listing where not isDraft {
    undeletable
}

expose transient shape Publish {
    listing: one Listing
}

rule ApplyPublish when Publish {
    listing.isDraft = false
}

-- ── Reports: evidence that outlives its subject ──────────────────────────────

-- A report references the living listing — correlation and traversal while it
-- lives — and COPIES the slice that must survive its deletion: the title is
-- durable business data; the reference serves the living target.
-- velle: absorbing reference plus per-field copy — the crux's resolution
-- (OQ37-R3); the copy is the author declaring, field by field, which slice of
-- the target was history all along
expose shape ListingReport {
    listing: one Listing? initially required
    listingTitle: text
    reason: text
    reportedOn: timestamp on create
}

-- The listing's deletion is the commit that flips this membership — entry is
-- ordinary drift, and NotifyReporter reacts to it. No `when deleted` trigger
-- exists: a business that must react to "this was deleted" models the state
-- the deletion leaves behind, or the deletion record below.
-- velle: deletion-caused drift entry (OQ37, "Refinements recalculate")
shape OrphanReport = ListingReport where listing is none

shape ReporterNotice {
    report: one ListingReport
    listingTitle: text
    noticedOn: timestamp on create
}

rule NotifyReporter when (OrphanReport where not exists ReporterNotice for this) {
    ReporterNotice from { report: this, listingTitle: listingTitle }
}

-- ── Discarding a draft ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- The request is transient — its durable trace is what was done, not the
-- asking. The outcome record is the explicitly modeled deletion record: what
-- a deletion leaves behind is the author's call, never minted for free.
-- velle: `delete` as an ordinary rule effect; the transient-acts copy idiom
-- as the deletion record (OQ37-R6: no built-in tombstone)
expose transient shape DiscardDraft {
    listing: one Listing
    reason: text
}

shape DiscardRecord {
    listingTitle: text
    reason: text
    discardedOn: timestamp on create
}

shape DiscardRefusal {
    listingTitle: text
    reason: text
    refusedOn: timestamp on create
}

-- The deleting rule rides the deletable partition; the complement rule makes
-- the refusal data (patterns.md, "Validation rejection is data"). Within the
-- deleting transaction the listing is fully present — ApplyDiscard reads its
-- title as one of its last readers; at transaction close it is removed.
-- velle: the partition idiom discharging the V27 gate; last-reader semantics
-- (OQ37, "Semantics of the deleting commit"); V18 totality by complement pair
rule ApplyDiscard when (DiscardDraft where listing is Draft) {
    DiscardRecord from { listingTitle: listing.title, reason: reason }
    delete listing
}

rule RefuseDiscard when (DiscardDraft where not (listing is Draft)) {
    DiscardRefusal from { listingTitle: listing.title, reason: reason }
}

-- ── Closing a seller's account ───────────────────────────────────────────────

expose transient shape CloseAccount {
    seller: one Seller
}

rule ApplyClose when CloseAccount {
    delete seller
}

-- The orphan side is the point, not a residue: "what does the business do
-- with a listing whose seller is gone?" is a real product question the
-- partition makes visible, where `?.` everywhere would have silently
-- propagated it away. Orphaned drafts are swept at the seller's deletion
-- commit — deletion-caused drift cascading as visible rules, each hop its own
-- decision; published listings stay, undeletable, awaiting product policy.
-- velle: the named-partition read discipline (OQ37-R10); a deleter fired by
-- another deleter's drift; `delete this` as the structural disarm
shape OrphanedListing = Listing where seller is none

rule SweepOrphanedDrafts when (OrphanedListing where isDraft) {
    delete this
}
